  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "\"Eid Mubarak\" is an Arabic phrase used by Muslims to mean \"blessed feast\" or \"blessed festival\". It is a traditional, festive greeting used to wish someone a happy holiday, specifically during the celebrations of Eid al-Fitr (marking the end of Ramadan) and Eid al-Adha. Key Details About \"Eid Mubarak\" Meaning: \"Eid\" means \"feast\" or \"festival,\" and \"Mubarak\" means \"blessed\" or \"congratulations\". Usage: It is used as a formal greeting to share joy, gratitude, and well wishes with family, friends, and community members during the Islamic holidays. Timing: While commonly used for both major Islamic holidays, it is frequently heard during Eid al-Fitr, which celebrates the end of the fasting month of Ramadan, say this article from The Columbus Dispatch. Alternatives: Other ways to express similar sentiments include \"Eid Saeed,\" which translates to \"Happy Eid\". The greeting is a cultural tradition rather than a strict religious obligation, and it is common for non-Muslims to use it to wish their Muslim friends a happy celebration.",
